WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 8 Leader Video
Unit 2, Lesson 4: Naturalism

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.570
So we've talked about worldviews, but what worldviews should we talk about?

2
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.510
We've talked about what worldviews are, but what worldviews should we actually talk

3
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.370
about? What worldviews are your students most exposed to?

4
00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.750
Now there's a lot. We're only going to touch a couple of the most popular

5
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:27.830
worldviews. And we're going to talk about some of the more dominant ones as well as

6
00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.870
what a biblical worldview starts to look like.

7
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.250
But the first one that we're going to talk about is the idea of naturalism.

8
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:41.070
Now, you can tell right out of the gate probably the idea of naturalism has the idea

9
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.650
of nature. Think of your senses.

10
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.690
Think of the five senses that you have, what you can taste, touch, smell, and feel.

11
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:53.630
Naturalism teaches that only those things are what constitutes reality.

12
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.310
And there we're back to that word again.

13
00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.830
What is reality? So when we're talking about these other worldviews, they're doing

14
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.670
the same thing that we're trying to do.

15
00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.510
Trying to talk about what is reality and what is truth.

16
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.110
How do we interpret these things in light of what naturalism actually is?

17
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.850
So those who believe in naturalism are trying to take the five senses and interpret

18
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.790
reality and dictate what is true based on those factors.

19
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.930
That's essentially what they're doing.

20
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.770
And this really, if you think about your students, especially those in public

21
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.710
schools this is exactly what they're being exposed to every day of the week is this

22
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:40.210
idea of naturalism especially those students that are in stem research type classes

23
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.530
science math etc technology those kinds of things because the sciences are very much

24
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:50.070
focused on the idea of what we can observe what we can see what we can touch what we

25
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.950
can taste what we can feel those kinds of things.

26
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.870
But also what they're trying to do is they're trying to not only figure out reality

27
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.090
and truth, but they're trying to figure out meaning.

28
00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.150
I mean, naturally life has meaning.

29
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.750
And this is where it gets a little bit tricky, is the naturalism point of view, the

30
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.750
naturalism worldview is trying to figure out, well, what does it mean to have

31
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.450
meaning in life?

32
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:23.310
Well, that's sort of sticky because naturalism actually can't do that.

33
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:29.310
From the viewpoint of naturalism being told that any meaning in life has to be found

34
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:35.250
then through science. So science is the only one that can determine what

35
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.290
kind of meaning there is.

36
00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:44.070
But science can only take us through the natural world and

37
00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.610
doesn't consider anything else. So we'll get to more of that in a second.

38
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.330
But what I want you to get, though, is that this lesson is more than just

39
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.650
understanding what naturalism is.

40
00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.630
It's being able to see that it can't work

41
00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.510
and that what we want our students to be able to determine is that they're not going

42
00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:08.270
to get away from it because it's the dominant view of Western culture, but to be

43
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.250
able to see things through it and interpret things in light of it and understand

44
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.670
that naturalism might be trying to do these things, it might be trying to explain

45
00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.390
meaning through science and the natural world, the natural order of things and all

46
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.430
that. But at the end of the day, it doesn't really work.

47
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:23.450
Why?

48
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:31.590
Well, because naturalism is most closely associated with the idea of atheism,

49
00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.990
which means no God, it

50
00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.950
cannot explain two fundamental questions.

51
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.050
It can't explain the universe in terms of how and

52
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:53.520
it can't explain things in terms of why now science is

53
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.220
trying to do this right science is trying to figure out how the universe was created

54
00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.940
how things got here and how it is that we're all here but science

55
00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:09.120
can't do this it can't determine why?

56
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.320
Is it just random? And if it's random, why is it random?

57
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.120
We could keep asking that why question over and over and over again.

58
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.880
And to dig a little bit deeper, it also can't do things like determine justice,

59
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.920
love, morality.

60
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.840
How do we determine those things?

61
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.380
How do we bring up the reality that there is justice in the world?

62
00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:40.920
How do we bring up the reality that two people love each other, and that we should

63
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.360
have love for our fellow humans and humankind.

64
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.580
Why is killing somebody else wrong, even if it might benefit me?

65
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.640
Why is stealing wrong? Why do we understand some morals to be

66
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.660
universal for all cultures of all people of all time?

67
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.880
Where do we get these things from?

68
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.660
See, this is where naturalism falls short, because when we're talking about things

69
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.660
like justice, love, and morality, these aren't tangible things that I can grab hold

70
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:18.400
of. These are not part of what I can taste, touch, smell, and physically

71
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.600
feel. I can't do that with justice necessarily.

72
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.040
I can't really do that with morality.

73
00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:30.200
These are things that are deeper than what is on the surface and require a deeper

74
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:36.040
explanation. So again it's important for

75
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.040
us to understand not only what naturalism actually is but it's also important for us

76
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.860
to understand that naturalism doesn't actually work so in your small groups

77
00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.720
especially in your small groups make sure you take the time to allow students

78
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.940
to sort of discover and discuss how naturalism sort of manifests itself in western

79
00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.960
culture what does that look like?

80
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.620
Where do they see that in their schools and in their classes?

81
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:03.940
It's not just

82
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.840
evolutionary science and those kinds of things.

83
00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:10.920
It might be deeper than that. So get your students to sort of have those discussions

84
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.760
to sort of get them to see beyond just the surface of what naturalism is, and have

85
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.800
them start to analyze it in terms of reality and truth.

86
00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:25.920
One thing great about this Bible study is that we dive into Ecclesiastes and the

87
00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.600
things that Solomon wrote. And I think he had this spot on, and it just brings so

88
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:35.880
much more to the surface when we consider this book in light of naturalism.

89
00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.020
He writes in the very, very beginning, right out of the gate, Meaningless,

90
00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.820
meaningless, says the teacher, utterly meaningless.

91
00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.220
Everything is meaningless.

92
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.200
What does man gain from all his labor at which he toils under the sun?

93
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:55.000
Generations come and generations go, but the earth remains forever.

94
00:06:55.820 --> 00:07:01.700
And what he's getting at throughout this whole book is the idea that

95
00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:06.580
without God putting meaning in life, life is meaningless.

96
00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:11.700
That all of our work and all of our efforts and everything that we stand for is

97
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.420
meaningless. So think about it from a naturalistic perspective.

98
00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:22.480
Without God, what is justice? Without God, what is love?

99
00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.340
Without God, what is morality?

100
00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.920
Why are we working besides to make money why is there any non-profit organization at

101
00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.540
all why do we care about clean water around the world why do we care about any of

102
00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:42.060
those things because from a naturalistic worldview there is no meaning behind those

103
00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:46.860
things there's no point to doing it because we're all just going to die and be dust

104
00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.600
and everything is going to go away and we cease to exist.

105
00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:55.960
But because we have the inclination to want to provide

106
00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:01.240
clean water, because we feel a sense of justice, because we understand certain

107
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:06.380
morals are universal, because we love one another and we're called to love one

108
00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.980
another, we understand that there's got to be something greater beyond than just the

109
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.360
natural world. There has to be the supernatural world.

110
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.600
There has to be a Creator God behind all of it.

111
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:24.100
So we see so clearly, and I want your students to see this as well, that

112
00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:28.880
when we talk about naturalism, it might sound so easy and so simple.

113
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.540
When we talk about that big buffet of worldviews, it might sound like, oh yeah, this

114
00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:38.940
idea works really well. But when they go down that buffet and they decide that, you

115
00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:42.360
know, yeah, yeah, I believe in naturalism and I don't believe in God, but I believe

116
00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:47.320
in taking care of orphans and widows and providing clean water to children around

117
00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.800
the world that don't have it. Those two things are now inconsistent.

118
00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.700
Those two things no longer make sense together.

119
00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:57.980
It's a great thing that you want to provide that for those people, but why do you

120
00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:01.980
want to do that? So this goes back to what we talked about last week with the idea

121
00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.660
of it has to be consistent. So if you're going to desire to want to take care of

122
00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:11.500
your fellow man and take care of people who can't take care of themselves.

123
00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.600
If you have that sense of justice and you have that sense of morality,

124
00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:19.400
why?

125
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.960
Again, it takes us back to here and naturalism can't answer that.

126
00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.580
So naturally what we would say is that your worldview then needs to adjust.

127
00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.640
So help your students navigate this.

128
00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:34.120
This is a little bit tricky and this, you know, requires some good discussions.

129
00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:39.500
Again, we'll keep reminding you always, always, always let the discussion flow, let

130
00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:43.720
students be honest about what they think, but challenge them in their thinking.

